      <content textType="body" fontSize="11" fontFamily="Roboto Mono" color="rgba(61,74,92,1)" lineSpacing="multiple:1.32" textAlign="left">
        <p>public interface PaymentValidationRule {</p>
        <p>  Set&lt;String&gt; validate(PostPaymentRequest request);</p>
        <p>}</p>
        <p>@Component</p>
        <p>public class CardNumberValidationRule</p>
        <p>    implements PaymentValidationRule {</p>
        <p>  public Set&lt;String&gt; validate(PostPaymentRequest r) {</p>
        <p>    // Luhn check, length, digits only</p>
        <p>  }</p>
        <p>}</p>
        <p>// Spring injects every rule - adding one</p>
        <p>// is simply adding a class</p>
        <p>private final List&lt;PaymentValidationRule&gt; rules;</p>
      </content>
    </shape>
    <shape type="text" topLeftX="604" topLeftY="92" width="300" height="250">
      <content textType="body" fontSize="13.5" fontFamily="Inter" color="rgba(61,74,92,1)" lineSpacing="multiple:1.4" textAlign="left">
        <ul>
          <li><p>Strategy pattern: one check per class, self-registered by Spring - no registry to maintain.</p></li>
          <li><p>All errors are collected, not fail-fast - one 400 instead of several round trips.</p></li>
          <li><p>Pure functions: no side effects, no state - trivially unit-testable.</p></li>
          <li><p>Open / Closed: a new rule touches nothing else.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </content>
    </shape>
